<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    {{! While an install request is pending or running, keep the page following it. A plain meta
        refresh (the Storage page's pattern) breaks here: the app container restarts mid-update, and
        a refresh that fires during that gap navigates to the browser's connection-error page and
        stops refreshing — stranding the admin. So with JS, poll /healthz and only reload once the
        app answers: the page rides through the restart and comes back showing the outcome. The meta
        refresh remains as the noscript fallback (its copy warns about the connection error). }}
    {{#updateAutoRefresh}}
    <noscript><meta http-equiv="refresh" content="4"></noscript>
    <script>
        setInterval(function () {
            fetch('/healthz', {cache: 'no-store'})
                .then(function (r) { if (r.ok) { window.location.reload(); } })
                .catch(function () { /* app restarting; keep polling until it answers again */ });
        }, 4000);
    </script>
    {{/updateAutoRefresh}}
    <title>Admin | Updates | {{siteName}}</title>
    {{>partials/head-deps.hbs}}
    {{>partials/head-styles.hbs}}
    {{! The manual-upgrade card collapses via Bootstrap, i.e. via JS. Without JS its toggle can't run,
        so leave the steps open instead of unreachable — a failed automatic update is exactly when an
        admin needs them. The id beats Bootstrap's .collapse:not(.show) on specificity. }}
    <noscript><style>#manualUpgradeSteps { display: block; }</style></noscript>
</head>
<body>
{{>partials/admin-shell.hbs}}
    <h1>Updates</h1>

    {{#updatePending}}
    <div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
        <span class="spinner-border spinner-border-sm" role="status" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <b>Update requested</b>; should begin within a few seconds.
        {{#pendingRequestedLine}}{{pendingRequestedLine}}{{/pendingRequestedLine}}
        <div class="small mt-1">This page will update automatically as upgrade progresses.</div></div>
    {{/updatePending}}

    {{#updateUnclaimed}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-timer-alert-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        The last install request was never picked up by the updater. Check
        <code>docker compose logs wikikt-updater</code> on the server; if an update is still
        available below, you can request it again.</div>
    {{/updateUnclaimed}}

    {{#updateRunning}}
    <div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
        <span class="spinner-border spinner-border-sm" role="status" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <b>Update in progress</b> ({{runPhase}}). {{runMessage}}
        <div class="small mt-1">Status will update automatically and show success or failure when finished.
        <noscript>Without JavaScript, expect a brief connection error while WikiKT restarts. Wait a
        minute, then reload this page for the result.</noscript></div></div>
    {{/updateRunning}}

    {{#updateAbandoned}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-help-circle-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        The last update's status is unknown — the updater stopped reporting mid-run. Check
        <code>docker compose logs wikikt-updater</code> on the server.</div>
    {{/updateAbandoned}}

    {{! Running build info -- always shown, whatever the check state. }}
    <div class="card mb-4">
        <div class="card-body d-flex align-items-center gap-3">
            <i class="mdi mdi-package-variant-closed fs-1 text-secondary" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <div>
                {{! The separator leads the optional part in each line, so a missing stamp (dev build)
                    or sha (source build without git) can't leave a dangling middot. }}
                <div><span title="App version" class="fw-bold">WikiKT {{appVersion}}</span>{{#appBuiltAt}} &middot; <span title="Build time (UTC)">{{appBuiltAt}}</span>{{/appBuiltAt}}</div>
                <div class="small text-secondary">{{#appGitSha}}<span title="Git commit">{{appGitSha}}</span> &middot; {{/appGitSha}}<a href="{{releasesUrl}}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Release history</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {{! Result of the last finished update. Colour and icon come from the model (phase-derived), and
        Dismiss hides it for good: nothing else would otherwise clear it (bar requesting the next
        update), so a one-time "updated successfully" note would live on this page forever.
        Dismissing records just this
        run's identity -- the updater's status.json (which the app mounts read-only) and
        `docker compose logs wikikt-updater` keep the full record, and the next result shows unread. }}
    {{#lastOutcome}}
    <div class="alert {{outcomeAlertClass}} alert-dismissible" role="alert"><i class="mdi {{outcomeIcon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {{outcomeMessage}} {{#outcomeRequestedLine}}{{outcomeRequestedLine}}{{/outcomeRequestedLine}}
        {{#outcomeFailed}}{{#outcomeBackupPath}}<div class="small mt-1">Pre-update backup: <code>{{outcomeBackupPath}}</code> (inside the <code>wikikt_update_state</code> volume).</div>{{/outcomeBackupPath}}{{/outcomeFailed}}
        {{! A form, not Bootstrap's data-bs-dismiss: the hide has to survive a reload. }}
        <form method="post" action="/a/updates/dismiss">
            {{{csrfField}}}
            <button type="submit" class="btn-close" aria-label="Dismiss this result" title="Dismiss this result"></button>
        </form>
    </div>
    {{/lastOutcome}}

    {{#stateDevBuild}}
    <div class="alert alert-secondary" role="alert">
        You are using a development build (<code>{{appVersion}}</code>). Automatic check for updates disabled; only stamped
        release builds have a published version to compare against.</div>
    {{/stateDevBuild}}

    {{#stateConsent}}
    <div class="card border-primary mb-4">
        <div class="card-header"><i class="mdi mdi-update" aria-hidden="true"></i> Check for new releases?</div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>WikiKT can check for new releases when you open this page (at most once a day), and show
                an "update available" note on the Administration dashboard (checked at most weekly).
                This sends an anonymous HTTPS request to <code>api.github.com</code> — no version,
                hostname, or other information about your site is ever sent. Nothing is requested until
                you enable it here.</p>
            <form method="post" action="/a/updates/settings" class="d-inline">
                {{{csrfField}}}
                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary" name="updateChecks" value="enable">Enable automatic update checks</button>
                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-outline-secondary" name="updateChecks" value="disable">Keep disabled</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    </div>
    {{/stateConsent}}

    {{#stateDeclined}}
    <div class="alert alert-secondary" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-bell-off-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        Automatic checks for updates are disabled.
        <form method="post" action="/a/updates/settings" class="d-inline ms-2">
            {{{csrfField}}}
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary" name="updateChecks" value="enable">Enable automatic update checks</button>
        </form>
    </div>
    {{/stateDeclined}}

    {{#stateUpToDate}}
    <div class="alert alert-success" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-check-circle-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        You are running the latest release. Checked {{checkedAt}}.</div>
    {{/stateUpToDate}}

    {{#stateFailed}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-alert-circle-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {{failMessage}} Last attempt {{checkedAt}}. If this server has no outbound internet access, use
        <b>Disable update checks</b> below to silence this.</div>
    {{/stateFailed}}

    {{#stateAvailable}}
    <div class="alert alert-info" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-arrow-up-circle-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <b>WikiKT {{latestVersion}} is available</b>. You are running {{appVersion}}.
        <a href="{{latestUrl}}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="alert-link">View release details</a>. Last checked: {{checkedAt}}.</div>

    {{#canInstall}}
    <div class="card border-primary mb-4">
        <div class="card-header"><i class="mdi mdi-rocket-launch-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i> Install automatically</div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>The updater will {{^dbIsFile}}take a database backup, {{/dbIsFile}}pull the new image,
                restart WikiKT, verify the new version reports healthy, and roll back automatically if it
                doesn't. WikiKT will restart partway through (a breif connection error on this page is normal).</p>
            {{#dbIsFile}}
            {{! The updater's automatic backup is a pg_dump against a database service. On H2 the
                database is a file in the app volume, so there is no service to dump and that step is
                skipped -- making the admin's own backup the only one that exists. }}
            <div class="alert alert-warning py-2" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-database-alert-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                This instance uses H2, so the updater <b>cannot</b> take its automatic pre-update database
                backup. Take a <a href="/a/backup/export" class="alert-link">full backup</a> first for safety,
                as there is no automatic rollback in case of failure.</div>
            {{/dbIsFile}}
            {{! onsubmit: instant feedback on the click (the button has no name/value, so disabling
                it mid-submit never drops a form field). The real "it started" state is the pending
                card the redirect lands on, derived from request.json — this is just the first paint. }}
            <form method="post" action="/a/updates/install"
                  onsubmit="var b = this.querySelector('button[type=submit]'); b.disabled = true; b.textContent = 'Requesting…';">
                {{{csrfField}}}
                <div class="form-check mb-2">
                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" name="confirmInstall" id="confirmInstall" value="1">
                    <label class="form-check-label" for="confirmInstall">I understand, and I have a recent
                        <a href="/a/backup/export">backup</a>.</label>
                </div>
                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary"><i class="mdi mdi-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Install {{latestVersion}}</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    </div>
    {{/canInstall}}

    {{#installMinUpgradeBlocked}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-stairs-up" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        This release upgrades cleanly only from version {{installMinUpgradeFrom}} or newer; upgrade to
        that version first (see the release notes). One-click install is disabled.</div>
    {{/installMinUpgradeBlocked}}
    {{#installComposeRevBlocked}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-file-alert-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        This release requires a Compose file change that the automatic update service cannot handle on its own.
        Follow the release notes and upgrade manually. One-click install is disabled for this release.</div>
    {{/installComposeRevBlocked}}
    {{#installNotSelfUpdatable}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-hand-back-left-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        This release is marked as requiring a manual upgrade; follow the release notes.</div>
    {{/installNotSelfUpdatable}}
    {{#installNoManifest}}
    <p class="form-text">This release has no published update manifest. One-click install is not available.</p>
    {{/installNoManifest}}
    {{#updaterStale}}
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert"><i class="mdi mdi-heart-off-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        The updater container isn't responding. Check <code>docker compose logs wikikt-updater</code> on your
        server. One-click install is unavailable until it reports in.</div>
    {{/updaterStale}}

    {{! Collapsed by default (Bootstrap collapse): these steps are the fallback, not the normal path,
        and they're long enough to bury the Install card above them. }}
    <div class="card mb-4">
        <div class="card-header">
            <button class="card-collapse-toggle" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                    data-bs-target="#manualUpgradeSteps" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="manualUpgradeSteps">
                <i class="mdi mdi-console" aria-hidden="true"></i> How to upgrade manually
                <i class="mdi mdi-chevron-down card-collapse-caret" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </button>
        </div>
        <div class="collapse" id="manualUpgradeSteps">
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-2">Use these steps for <span class="fw-semibold">manual upgrades</span> if automatic is not available or desired.</p>
            <p class="mb-2">Take a <a href="/a/backup/export">backup</a> first. Then, on the server, from the
                directory holding your Compose file, perform one of the following sets of steps, depending on how
                you installed WikiKT in the first place:</p>
            <p class="mb-1 fw-semibold">Using the prebuilt image (the default production setup):</p>
            <pre class="bg-body-tertiary p-2 rounded"><code>sudo docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml pull wikikt
sudo docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d</code></pre>
            <p class="mb-1 fw-semibold">Building from source (if your Compose file uses <code>build: .</code>):</p>
            <pre class="bg-body-tertiary p-2 rounded"><code>git pull
sudo docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build wikikt</code></pre>
            <p class="form-text {{#updaterInstalled}}mb-0{{/updaterInstalled}}">Running <code>docker-compose.home.yml</code> or a plain jar instead? See the upgrade
                notes in <code>docker/README.md</code> and <code>docs/install.md</code>. Database migrations run
                automatically at startup; database, uploads, and certificates persist in their volumes.</p>
            {{^updaterInstalled}}
            <p class="form-text mb-0">Prefer one-click updates from this page? Add
                <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES=selfupdate</code> to <code>.env</code> and re-run
                <code>docker compose up -d</code> — see "One-click updates" in <code>docker/README.md</code>
                (it holds the Docker socket, so read the trust note there first).</p>
            {{/updaterInstalled}}
        </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    {{/stateAvailable}}

    {{#checksEnabled}}
    <div class="d-flex gap-2 align-items-center">
        <form method="post" action="/a/updates/check">
            {{{csrfField}}}
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-outline-secondary"><i class="mdi mdi-refresh" aria-hidden="true"></i> Check now</button>
        </form>
        <form method="post" action="/a/updates/settings">
            {{{csrfField}}}
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-link text-secondary" name="updateChecks" value="disable">Disable update checks</button>
        </form>
    </div>
    {{/checksEnabled}}
{{>partials/admin-shell-end.hbs}}
{{{bodyHtml}}}</body>
</html>
